so this month i'm focusing a little bit  on increasing my focus so therefore i  read all books on motivation and focus  and how to increase those and i found  this book called the one thing that i  really liked and with this book i  learned a few lessons that hopefully you  can apply as well if you're doing your  phd right now or if you're a student and  let's get right into it so hi i'm  schloss fraser a phd student in  computational neuroscience at the donors  institute in the netherlands and today i  want to talk about the four lessons i  learned from this book called the one  thing and i think in general it is a  really enjoyable book so if you want to  learn a little bit more i also highly  recommend to read it but let's get  straight into the first lesson that i  learned so the first thing the book  talks about is the myth of to-do lists  and i actually really like this because  the idea is that to-do lists if you make  them you just put everything that you  have to do in that day but the pitfall  with to-do list that i've also noticed  in myself is that it's really easy to  just start doing these tiny little tasks  that clutter up your to-do list like for  example replying to emails or doing the  grocery shopping or texting back this  one friend and although these are valid  things you have to do in a day these are  not the most important thing and  especially not for your research or if  you're a student for studying and it's  really easy to feel really good at the  end of the day if you crossed off all  these tiny to-do's but you haven't  really done anything significant for  your major goals which are your research  or finishing your studies and therefore  i think this is a really valid point  that the myth of to-do list that they  make you really productive so the book  then goes into the burrito principle and  the burrito principle is that eighty  percent of all your output is actually  produced by just 20 of your input so  probably if you're studying all day all  the studying is  mainly a lot of reading or a lot of  looking at lectures online but the  actual studying where you recall the  information is probably only about two  hours every day if you study for 10  hours and the same thing is if you're  doing research right now i notice it  with myself that i can spend a lot of  time watching lectures or reading papers  but the actual work that i need to do is  usually only around two hours every day  so with the burrito principle you kind  of if you put all the tasks you need to  do you can kind of see which are the 20  that are actually giving the results and  once you've found those you can focus  more on those and the book the one thing  actually goes further and they say that  you really have to zoom in even more and  find every day that one thing that you  can do in your project or in your  company that will give you the most  return value and i do think in a sense  this is a little bit an  oversimplification because of course  there are many things you could do or  many things that would be beneficial but  i do think in a period when you're  feeling a little bit overwhelmed and  you're not really sure which way to go  should i go to b or a and you're not  making any choices then i think it's  really good to focus down on just one  thing and that's why this week i try to  do that because i am feeling a little  bit overwhelmed in my research right now  because there are just so many  possibilities i could go  towards so yeah i decided every day in  the morning to just pick one thing i had  to do that day and which  would be the most important for my  research and then only focus on  completing that one task and in the  afternoon or in the evening if i  finished it i could do anything i want  so i could watch some lectures or i  could also have some leisure time if i  wanted to and i do think this helped a  lot like it made me feel at least a  little bit accomplished every day and  kind of gave me that dopamine boost that  i needed to continue my research so the  second lesson he goes into is the myth  of willpower and i personally really  agree with this and you probably noticed  it as well that if you have to use your  willpower a lot during the day it's  actually depleted quite quickly so if  you want to do many things that require  willpower during the day the probability  of you doing those things late in the  day are very slim to none  so if you think of your tasks and you  see a lot of them require willpower you  have to kind of calculate this in and  also for me the thing i noticed the most  is that because i'm working at home in  my office all day there's so much  temptation to just get a snack or to  watch youtube for a little bit and to  resist this actually requires a lot of  willpower so that's why next week mainly  i want to see if i can change up my  environment a little bit because even  though university is closed right now  where i live the cafes are still open or  i could go to friends and i think by  being around people that are also a  little bit focused i need to use less of  my willpower to stay focused and the  third lesson i learned from this book is  the myth of a work-life balance so the  fact with balance is that we kind of  feel that once we've achieved balance  then we're there we're set we're good to  go we have achieved the pinnacle of life  but the thing is with balance that you  can kind of go in and out of it quite  quickly so for example if you do yoga  and you do the three pose then you know  that staying in the tree pose actually  requires a lot of mental effort and also  for your body to keep adjusting itself  to keep standing and the same thing is  with work life balance he explains in  the book so work life balance is a  constant struggle between leaning too  much in to your work at periods of time  and leaning too much into your personal  relationships or for example your  hobbies and i think this is true i think  there are just periods in your life that  you are more focused on your work or  you're a little bit stressed for your  studies and you need to study for a  continuous week but then it's really  important to also go back to being a  little bit more focused on your own  personal life so for example if you have  a stressful period for one month it's  really nice to then block in a small  holiday or or a longer weekend that you  can really reconnect with your hobbies  or with your friends and that is kind of  what balance is so balance is not really  set on an everyday level and it's also  different per individual so some people  need to see a lot of people or have a  lot of relationships whereas other  people  are okay to work actually quite a lot  and don't  need that much balance in that sense but  one thing he also talks about which i  thought was really good is that if life  is kind of like juggling all these  different facets in our life so for  example work friends family your health  then work is kind of like a rubber ball  if you drop it during the juggling it  will bounce back up if you pick it up  again but family and friends in your  health are kind of like glass balls so  if you drop them they actually scatter  or shatter and i think this is actually  really true because you cannot just  leave your friends and your family for  one year and expect them to just be  waiting for you unless you have really  good friends or family but these are  actually continuous relationships that  you need to work on so for example if  you have children if you miss the first  years of their life you cannot really  get those back whereas with work i think  you can always start again and but this  could be personal i think just in the  end life is not work you really it's  more relationships and friends and  family and also your health so these are  things you should never fully drop even  during periods that you feel you need to  work really hard so yeah and then the  last thing he talks about is to find  that one thing that you can do and i  think he recommends a really interesting  exercise and it kind of goes like this  so you think of the next five years  what's the one thing you want to do that  you think will change your life so for  me that's of course getting my phd  finally oh my god and then the next step  is to go a little bit smaller so what's  the one thing you can do this year that  will help you achieve that goal so for  me it would be something like writing  one paper this year although the year is  almost over so i won't finish that but  next year and then you go even smaller  so what's the one thing you can do this  month that will help you achieve this  goal what's the one thing you can do  this week what's the one thing you can  do this day and then what's the one  thing you can do this hour and i think  this is a really interesting exercise  that you can do with every point of your  life that you find important so for  example you could also think of friends  and family what's the thing i want to do  the next five years that would really  increase my value of the relationships  that i have and then go smaller like  that and i think by going through this  exercise everything that you kind of  want in life becomes suddenly very  tangible and very reachable and that's  why i do think it's a really nice  exercise so yeah to try to find your one  thing and if you're struggling a little  bit with motivation and feeling a little  bit burned out during your phd i did  make another video about it so you can  check that out right here and otherwise  see you next week for another focus  motivation video so see you bye
 

